# KNOWN GAP. `@derive(Hashable)` on a struct with a `float` field produces a
# LINK FAILURE: the generated `K__hash` calls `double__hash`, which nothing
# defines. Eight lines of ordinary Gorget, `gg check` clean.
#
# Measured 2026-08-19 at HEAD:
#     gg check  -> "OK: no semantic errors"   (rc 0)
#     gg build  -> dh.c:(.text.K__hash+0x50): undefined reference to `double__hash'
#                  collect2: error: ld returned 1 exit status
#
# The user's diagnostic for deriving a standard trait on a standard scalar field
# is a linker error naming a mangled symbol they never wrote. Same class as the
# `equip T(Trait):` mis-spelling already filed: `gg check` accepts, the failure
# surfaces at link, and the message points at compiler-internal naming.
#
# ⚠ The int-field sibling works, so this is a per-scalar-type hole in the
# derive's hash-helper table, not a broken derive: swap `float f` for `int i`
# and the same program builds and prints `1`.
#
# INTENDED: prints `1`. Either the derive emits a `double__hash` helper, or
# `gg check` REJECTS the derive with a teaching diagnostic naming the field
# whose type has no hash helper. A link error is neither.
#
# Wired test: known_gap_derive_hashable_float_field_link_failure
# (tests/integration.rs, #[ignore]d).
@derive(Equatable, Hashable)
struct K:
    float f

void main():
    Set[K] s = Set[K]()
    s.add(K(1.5))
    print(s.len())
